#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158408} :activity/id #uuid "d27a6ca7-a613-5fcb-81cc-fb3bce2834e6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049028 :topic/id #uuid "d4a63775-e398-5313-94f3-82976001242f" :topic/math-academy-id 2973} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158527 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we’ll learn another method for solving inhomogeneous linear ODEs: **variation of parameters** (VOP).\n\nRecall that a *first-order linear ODE* can be written in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nTo solve it, we look for a solution of the form\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c(x)$ is the *complementary solution* (solution to the homogeneous equation) and $y_p(x)$ is a *particular solution* (any one solution to the original equation).\n\nIn variation of parameters, we start with the homogeneous solution and then allow its constant to vary with $x.$\n\n- **Step 1:** Solve the corresponding homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nIts general solution has the form\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = {\\color{blue}C}e^{-\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n- **Step 2:** Guess the form of a particular solution by allowing the constant $C$ to depend on $x.$ \n\nWe now choose an **ansatz**, meaning an *assumed form* for a solution. In VOP, we assume\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = {\\color{blue}u(x)}e^{-\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(x)$ is an unknown function.\n\nIntuitively, $y_p(x)$ has the same form as the homogeneous solution $y_c(x),$ but its amplitude changes with $x.$\n\n- **Step 3:** Substitute $y_p(x)$ into the original ODE. \n\nUsing the product rule, we find the derivative:\n\n$$\ny_p' = u' e^{-\\int P\\,\\textrm d x} - u P e^{-\\int P\\,\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nWhen substituted into $y' + Py = Q,$ the second term above cancels with $Py_p,$ leaving\n\n$$\nu'(x)e^{-\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm d x} = Q(x).\n$$\n\nThis allows us to solve for $u'(x),$ integrate to find $u(x),$ and finally recover $y_p(x).$\n\nOn the next slide, we'll construct a suitable VOP ansatz for a particular first-order equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc027443-e6e3-5ff7-b9a5-f2b9022b4839" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4691 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0a16aabc-6ee5-5a4a-a2b0-bb3cdc618fdb" :step/math-academy-id 593160 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157051 :question/id #uuid "6a492cbf-d378-5193-baf2-c88a2bea63fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21774"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050392}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503705}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"} :step/id #uuid "bbeb54b8-b562-53e6-929d-77208cf333bb" :step/math-academy-id 593161 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158410}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158462 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + 3y = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nLet's solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nTo find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is the sum of $y_c$ and $y_p{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p = Ae^{-3x} + \\dfrac15 e^{2x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5fa47c37-0c4f-544e-acd7-42498caa07c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14600 :tutorial/title "Applying Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "08d84b91-6027-5011-a415-d1dd1299ecda" :step/math-academy-id 593164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157052 :question/id #uuid "9a26313d-4b93-5d82-98c1-dbecb0bead82" :question/math-academy-id "e-21775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508931}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "c9a509fa-55ef-5eca-8545-a3c0059a0a95" :step/math-academy-id 593165 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158463 :tutorial/content "Next, consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = e^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nTo find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. We call this a **resonant case** because the forcing term is proportional to the homogeneous solution $e^{5x}.$\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\n**Watch out!** Even in resonant cases, the VOP ansatz is unchanged. The only difference is that the resulting equation for $u'$ becomes especially simple. Let's see how.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We omit the constant of integration when solving for $u(x),$ since adding a constant to $u(x)$ would just add a multiple of the homogeneous solution.\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we identify $y_p$ and sum the solutions to find the general solution: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=A{e}^{5x}+x{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2f28a48b-0a76-5234-b54d-0c315719b855" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14601 :tutorial/title "Cases When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "8032045e-3df7-5ea2-a742-40454ef25928" :step/math-academy-id 593168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158413}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157053 :question/id #uuid "bafa3bf1-a867-5259-a6da-226195b45541" :question/math-academy-id "e-21777"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508968}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "b658e981-ab4e-578e-9e96-6d98a186e23b" :step/math-academy-id 593169}] :activity/title "Variation of Parameters for First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157522}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509015} :activity/id #uuid "c6c1a0ec-06d6-5126-929c-41d3f2b05197" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049028 :topic/id #uuid "d4a63775-e398-5313-94f3-82976001242f" :topic/math-academy-id 2973} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441fd9ba-02ad-5b58-b31f-7123f793b71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508489} {:answer/id #uuid "969dac44-be6b-55c3-9e32-bc9b239ac320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508615} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf4b20a-605e-5c2b-bb92-3273af82d50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508627} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd05d60-383c-5086-b2f2-a8d58e22437d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508804} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7bb3c4-5f99-5055-85cf-792009ea367b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad355eb-c779-513f-be3e-7ffebc0b039b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508597} {:answer/id #uuid "9a08cb88-e308-5923-8a7a-40f0260d87f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508621} {:answer/id #uuid "a2025edc-5ef5-5831-9d2e-09965e794712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508634} {:answer/id #uuid "bf951e29-b66d-506c-9922-457610fdd7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508597} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2b4430-672a-5bf3-807e-80a3453382c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051c9750-97c1-5281-b90e-9994fbef376b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508395} {:answer/id #uuid "7f15f83b-8c0f-5827-b511-b0cca04cf4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508575} {:answer/id #uuid "a8312813-e54b-52a6-b6b9-51feaefb1de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508644} {:answer/id #uuid "c8881434-0fe7-5f65-a6ba-6bbdbf42ac37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508703} {:answer/id #uuid "f55f4069-0947-5902-9b9e-4194006e3fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a17ac1c5-d149-5ebf-8927-158723ddb605" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fdf7d2e-21a5-5a00-afb9-716691a79836" :question/math-academy-id "q-337475" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3-3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3-3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3-3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2a815087-9d2f-5f79-be34-541c824a1cf8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1821fb92-1bc6-52cb-840b-fe52e847d9c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503031} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7ec567-b8e4-5e6b-b0e3-a54a500bf3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503040} {:answer/id #uuid "a7422c58-6999-5683-9ddd-d6ef6add5c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503319} {:answer/id #uuid "b4568322-688f-539c-8603-9923f672bd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503350} {:answer/id #uuid "c0337af8-e9e5-53c9-b443-71e8a86df710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503365} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ee3d70-9cd7-5866-8013-20b03f25f1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503365} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ebff2a-086f-5eb7-8db3-ea98b1441d99" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503532} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1161af44-bb89-5c5f-aa0f-dfa35be44438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503019} {:answer/id #uuid "363e73c3-14d2-5aca-b3ba-4ef206b7ef08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503078} {:answer/id #uuid "732d4a6f-1012-548f-b6da-4987a64f221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503213} {:answer/id #uuid "f80b0984-cac5-5225-97ae-b6ef195606a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503491} {:answer/id #uuid "fc81d441-b5a7-5ee4-9c97-4238d1d269e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503491} :answer-field/id #uuid "920d47b2-3068-590e-abf0-2fae86113747" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503587} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ddfe20-b79c-5165-8331-392575132b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503026} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe7d00c-76aa-574c-969b-e41afc21f245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503168} {:answer/id #uuid "9694c5a6-3a43-5500-8040-5188f8885538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503279} {:answer/id #uuid "df205db7-4aa9-531f-bc0d-d78eb84cc322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503026} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2790fa-4124-595d-b202-1fbf531eba53" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6116c6c-0d2d-5d26-9e6b-b6dd0de87989" :question/math-academy-id "q-337452" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ce6f69a-e8bd-5bb3-8b7f-545dd51be273" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cccc3f-adc6-5111-9198-1a5b88a5a074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508427} {:answer/id #uuid "4f43de6f-04e2-5d63-90f9-1ce173750674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}(2-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508507} {:answer/id #uuid "60b25e8f-17ab-51aa-a03a-68dfa45df23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508534} {:answer/id #uuid "97600c60-53b5-5a9f-b766-554cc68ec812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508427} :answer-field/id #uuid "24592161-15b0-5490-bf70-ec314dc5ab32" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f468e91-dbc8-5d0c-b8a0-23f7ac7be778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508411} {:answer/id #uuid "76275a67-1e05-50cc-b6cb-419076dc72fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508560} {:answer/id #uuid "89ad4179-3b9d-5f19-97bc-571345ef606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508595} {:answer/id #uuid "c89cb2e5-dba3-52c9-90ee-7ceaad48af71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508411} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec757a1-adaa-50b6-9d63-cc3bd102c0d3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a6edef-23b0-5485-8d01-f1b5d32f3b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508422} {:answer/id #uuid "27ab71c1-e83e-5aaf-af19-b551cd5fa99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508452} {:answer/id #uuid "60ed5253-398b-5c0a-8f8d-ca5190048731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508535} {:answer/id #uuid "80497a1c-8142-5bdf-a1ab-0bf6183b5f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508579} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bef3d2-cd60-5852-b113-b1025b37297d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508723}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508579} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbadbf3f-b18a-5a99-9826-e9ff4c997442" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5753a7d8-bd1a-52e7-a471-c1acec6a4f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-352894" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8202d26a-ddb4-51a5-a248-916138bbdab9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509020}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36dc406b-5149-5aeb-99f4-c422c56adc09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397754} {:answer/id #uuid "11154d93-5ccc-5c68-a857-aee315edeb48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397755} {:answer/id #uuid "23e73190-9abd-5014-8776-a3e439817b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397756} {:answer/id #uuid "2db97ceb-c880-55bd-9dad-60e6eed828e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397757} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a467a6f-a5c6-5790-ac4d-f97c7812ce6a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c182f9-383c-58da-882f-395c04dd6b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397759} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e5a89f-4546-53e1-a022-5e7739f83b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397760} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec37a94-cd94-5e1f-98c5-54bc9d2c9cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(4x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397761} {:answer/id #uuid "dd232a56-f4cc-56fc-b884-30cf0430eb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1+4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397760} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fff85ae-4624-593f-9e66-64f227957388" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397758} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0512b396-796a-5a66-9e01-233e25cac296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397764} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e91fdf-1722-5c86-aba4-e7001a74c175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397765} {:answer/id #uuid "81b3d8a7-3cff-52cb-8e4e-a47b128cf79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397766} {:answer/id #uuid "37921492-3c46-559e-816c-ff8ee78351ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397767} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff85d2-c6bc-5e83-bcd4-df22d2bc5e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397767} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b6e071-99a9-577f-a558-4259b093492e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d21173e6-c5c3-5364-8e5b-d645bc7f05bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352895" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=1-4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d89a719-5776-5738-90c1-4252105b7daa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de0f2ba-ffe2-5a6b-89cd-556d8f691f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x+2{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508440} {:answer/id #uuid "572f375a-067a-5a7b-8a7d-bf629da1a48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x+{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508518} {:answer/id #uuid "e8298673-1ed0-5ba3-a20b-d11c59c9e2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+2x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508759} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a135e2-6bd6-52bb-b1b1-0b14d3f74e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508518} :answer-field/id #uuid "23bc5b9e-0309-5c0f-a261-a533ceac5bb2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1746bc-f878-52ec-b146-aec22500c4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508406} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb39d55-b678-52fb-8de4-aaf0efa68f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508443} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1a0ed5-39a5-5b49-889d-036a63baa72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508551} {:answer/id #uuid "dc95adb5-f92a-5a3d-adb4-eb25560bfcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508741} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34519d1-654b-50eb-a652-7f98090b4427" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275b8a15-bc0b-5e5c-86af-632ebcfba8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508451} {:answer/id #uuid "a1869588-4b98-59e1-96f4-ff1a600d8834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508632} {:answer/id #uuid "aacafe0e-5f45-594f-af93-bbdab4f15874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508648} {:answer/id #uuid "df1d3c99-484f-5c4a-b945-29f6e6aaa30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508744} {:answer/id #uuid "e228f210-a90d-5991-975d-f9c2f0b7416a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508748} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9324986-dc8e-56cf-affb-81d9274ea958" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "adce0112-6117-576f-afb6-900e0d57f067" :question/math-academy-id "q-337526" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\  & =16{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =8{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =8-8{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8-8{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8-8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =8x+{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d34432c1-07b0-58c4-a35d-f354ab6b00c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509019}}] :activity/title "Variation of Parameters for First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508385}]}
